{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794439976,"nonce":"O7qKHyCNtuKHNF8gey6BcyJNZWE=","message":"Committed as 98aae96, not pushed.\n\nA scriptable host builds on this Mac after all, and the generated binding passes from a script.\n\nThe zlib failure: hxcpp's zutil.h defines `fdopen` as a macro on a Mac only `#ifndef fdopen`, so a host compiled with `-Dfdopen=fdopen` gets past it. The fixture's host and the one the command writes carry that flag in their `@:buildXml`, for macOS only.\n\nWhat the first try showed that the issue's opening guess had wrong: a script cannot be compiled against the hxcpp classes themselves. Haxe still types them, and `cpp.vm.Gc.setFinalizer`, raw pointers and the extern class do not type or generate under `cppia`. So the generator writes declarations for a script: `hxn_gen::haxe_cppia`, one `extern class` an interface with its public methods (and `same`, `weak`, `close`), one a weak class, and the library's memory class with its meter. `hxn-gen` writes them to `haxe/cppia`. A unit test holds each declaration to the public functions of the hxcpp class of the same name.\n\nWhat a host needs:\n- `-D scriptable`, the binding's classes and `hxn` included whether used or not (`--macro include`), and `-D dll_export=\u003cfile\u003e` so a script's compile can leave out what the host holds (`-D dll_import=\u003cfile\u003e`).\n- `-dce no`. With the default, a script that called `haxe.Int64.make` failed to link at load: the host had dropped it as unused.\n\nBuilt:\n- tests/counter/run.sh gen-cppia: the host (tests/counter/haxe/cppia/Host.hx), haxe/Generated.hx compiled as a script, run in it. Everything Generated.hx checks passes: objects, shared values, atomics, lent functions, futures, streams, signals, numbers, the meter and the collector. The fixture's `Finish` class is held by the host, since a script cannot write C++.\n- `hxn build cppia` and `hxn run cppia`: the hxcpp build, then a host under `\u003ctarget\u003e/hxn/\u003clibrary\u003e/cppia`, the program compiled with `-cppia` and run by the host. The host shifts hxcpp's argument globals by one, so the script's `Sys.args()` are its own and not led by its file name. `haxe-flags` go to both compiles.\n- `hxn package cpp` also writes `binding/cppia`; `hxn.Bind.lib` adds it for a compile with `cppia` defined, and for an hxcpp compile with `scriptable` includes the binding's classes and `hxn`.\n- tests/cli/run.sh cppia and tests/examples/run.sh cppia. The first also builds a host and a script against the package alone.\n\nRun on macOS (hxcpp 4.3.2): tests/counter/run.sh gen-cppia, the script five and then three more times in the built host; tests/cli/run.sh cppia; tests/examples/run.sh cppia. Not run: Linux and Windows, which CI does for the fixture and the command's test once pushed; the examples as scripts are not in CI, each being a host to build.\n\nOpen:\n- A host takes about two minutes to build here and is rebuilt in full when a define changes.\n- A script that calls `Sys.exit` on Windows ends through the host's `exit`, with the hazard of issue 5c939da6cb118eabcbce8bd74dce1bd52549df123c28f506b3f7a9ccfcc294dd.\n- A path with a `'` in it breaks the `include` macro call the command passes.\n- A declaration is of the generated classes only. A class a library's author writes around them for a script is theirs to declare.","files":null}]}